--TEST--
What `status` answers and the case a program compares it against are one type, and it is not interchangeable with another `Core` enum of the same ordinals — `rule:concurrency/queue-four-members` and `rule:concurrency/attempts-are-finite-and-a-dead-letter-is-kept`, `rule:enums/closed-integer-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:enums/core-enums
// The agreement first, and it is the half that compiles: the answer and the
// case are the same type, so `$state == Core\Queue\State::Succeeded` is a
// comparison a program may write and not a cast it has to spell.
var $receipt = Core\Queue::push("jobs/send-receipt.nvs");

Core\Queue\State $state = Core\Queue::status($receipt);
$state = Core\Queue\State::Succeeded;

// And it is that enum and no other. `Core\Db\Isolation` is a `Core` enum too,
// with its own cases numbered from 0, so nothing whatever *but* the type stops
// the two being swapped — which is exactly what `rule:enums/closed-integer-type`'s closed named type
// is for, and what an `int` state column would have thrown away.
Core\Db\Isolation $wrong = $state;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Db\Isolation`, found `Core\Queue\State`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
